          title: "“In my Father’s house are many rooms”",
          body: "These are among the most comforting words ever spoken, and they are spoken to men whose hearts are breaking — Jesus is leaving, Peter will deny, all will scatter. Against that fear he sets a home. The **Father’s house** is heaven itself, and it has **many rooms** — the older word *mansions* is misleading; the picture is not of grand separate estates but of *abiding-places* (the noun is from the verb “to remain,” 15:4), room enough in the Father’s own dwelling for every one of his children, none crowded out. Notice the ground of the promise: it does not rest on our worthiness but on his *going.* **“I go to prepare a place for you”** — the cross and resurrection open the door; his departure that so grieves them is the very thing that makes room for them. And then the personal heart of it: **“I will come again and take you to myself, that where I am you also may be.”** Heaven is defined not as a place of golden streets but as *being with him* — the whole ache of the separation answered by the promise of unbroken presence. Whatever else is uncertain, the destination is a Person, and he himself is coming back to fetch us there.",
