      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a14.27.1',
          kind: "reading",
          refs: ['g14.27.1', 'g14.27.9', 'g14.27.22'],
          lemma: undefined,
          title: "“Peace I leave with you”",
          body: "*Shalom* was the ordinary word of greeting and farewell — *peace be with you* — but on Jesus’ lips at this hour it is filled to overflowing. He is bequeathing something, like a dying man leaving a legacy: **“my peace I give to you.”** Not peace in the world’s sense — the mere absence of trouble, a quiet that lasts only as long as circumstances behave — but *his own* peace, the settled, unshakable rest of one who is at home in the Father’s will even as he walks toward the cross. **“Not as the world gives do I give.”** The world’s peace is fragile because it depends on things going well; Christ’s peace holds *in the storm,* because it depends on the Father, who does not change. And so the chapter comes full circle: it opened *“Let not your hearts be troubled”* (v. 1) and closes with the same words plus one more — *“neither let them be afraid.”* But now the command has a gift behind it. He does not merely tell frightened men to stop being afraid; he hands them the very peace that makes the not-fearing possible. This is the deep logic of Christian courage: the calm is not summoned up from within but *received* from him, and kept by trusting the One who has overcome the world (16:33).",
        },
      ],
